What is the historical context and significance of the song 'The Mary Ellen Carter'?
The song "The Mary Ellen Carter" is an inspirational shanty written and first recorded by Stan Rogers, intended to convey the message of triumphing over great odds. The song tells the story of a heroic effort to salvage a sunken ship, the "Mary Ellen Carter", by some members of her crew. The song has become a tribute to Stan Rogers and has been sung to close the annual Winnipeg Folk Festival every year since his death.

The song gained significant historical context and significance when it was credited with saving the life of Robert M. "Bob" Cusick, the chief mate of the ship "Marine Electric", which sank in a storm in 1983. Cusick was trapped under the ship's wreckage and was able to survive by singing the song and holding his breath as the waves washed over him. His story was later told to Stan Rogers, who was deeply moved by the incident and invited Cusick to attend one of his concerts.

The song has since become a symbol of hope and resilience, and its message of "never giving up" has inspired many people. The fact that it has been sung at the Winnipeg Folk Festival every year since Rogers' death is a testament to its enduring significance and impact.
